🚀 Professional Profile
This repository showcases advanced proficiency in Deep Packet Inspection (DPI) and forensic analysis. My work focuses on the security of Legacy Systems—where modern security tools often 
fail—requiring a deep understanding of core protocols like NetBIOS, DHCP, and ARP to identify sophisticated threats such as DNS tunneling and data exfiltration.

🛡️ Strategic Project Scope
In this environment, I performed a comprehensive security audit of network traffic to identify indicators of compromise (IoC) and reconnaissance activities.

1. Intelligence-Driven Threat Hunting
Modern Protocol Forensics: Analyzed HTTP POST requests to identify potential credential harvesting or unauthorized data submissions to external endpoints.

DNS Data Exfiltration Detection: Identified anomalous DNS TXT/MX records with query lengths exceeding 240 bytes—a high-confidence indicator of data tunneling used by advanced persistent threats (APTs).

CyberChef Integration: Utilized industry-standard OSINT tools to "defang" malicious indicators, ensuring safe handling of threat intelligence.

2. Legacy System Visibility 
Legacy environments often rely on older broadcast and naming protocols. My analysis ensures these "blind spots" are monitored:

NBNS & NetBIOS Inspection: Monitored host registration and name queries for legacy windows environments to prevent NetBIOS poisoning attacks.

ARP & DHCP Forensics: Audited broadcast traffic and vendor class identifiers (e.g., android-dhcp-11) to detect unauthorized device insertion and rogue DHCP servers.

3. Attack Surface Mapping (Reconnaissance Defense)
Reconstruction of Scans: Successfully mapped a multi-stage reconnaissance effort by identifying TCP SYN floods and UDP port scans through ICMP Type 3 (Destination Unreachable) analysis.

🛠️ Technical Toolkit
Primary Tool: Wireshark (Certified Competent)

Specialized Filters:

dns.qry.name.len > 50 (Tunneling Detection)

tcp.flags.syn==1 && tcp.flags.ack==0 (Scanning Detection)

http.request.method == "POST" (Data Leak Prevention)

